# machine-guard :: allow list
#
# Extended-regex patterns matched against absolute paths. A finding whose path
# matches any line here is suppressed from signature/technique checks.
#
# Rationale: security tooling and incident documentation MUST contain the
# indicator strings in order to detect or describe them. A scanner that flags
# scanners is the single fastest way to train a team to ignore it -- which is
# exactly how Brain-PowerStation's malware-scan CI ended up failing on its own
# .husky/pre-push guard during a live incident.
#
# This list does NOT apply to:
#   - the integrity baseline (a change is a change, always reported)
#   - quarantine executable-bit checks (never suppressed)
# Keep it as tight as possible.

# This tool itself
/Users/peter/Documents/machine-guard/

# Claude skills: detection scripts, IoC catalogs, templates
/\.claude/skills/
/\.claude/projects/.*/memory/

# Defensive tooling inside repos
/\.husky/
/\.github/workflows/
/scripts/security/
/\.bin/local-security-guard\.sh$
/malware-scan\.(sh|yml)$
/infection-check\.sh$
/check-machine\.sh$

# Incident documentation
/docs/security/
/2026-q2-incident/
\.(md|txt|rst)$

# Package manager internals with legitimately long minified lines.
# NOTE: this suppresses the *long line* technique check only. npm's own
# entry points are covered far more strictly by the integrity baseline.
/node_modules/.*/dist/
/node_modules/undici/lib/llhttp/
/node_modules/(lru-cache|glob|tar|diff|qs|ipaddr\.js)/
